L000081	\title{Curvature Perturbations from First-Order Phase Transitions:
L000082	\texorpdfstring{\\}{}Implications to Black Holes and Gravitational Waves}
L000083	
L000084	\author{Gabriele Franciolini}
L000085	\email{gabriele.franciolini@cern.ch}
L000086	\affiliation{CERN, Theoretical Physics Department, Esplanade des Particules 1, Geneva 1211, Switzerland}
L000087	\author{Yann Gouttenoire}
L000088	\email{yann.gouttenoire@gmail.com}
L000089	\affiliation{School of Physics and Astronomy, Tel-Aviv University, Tel-Aviv 69978, Israel}
L000090	\affiliation{PRISMA+ Cluster of Excellence $\&$ MITP, Johannes Gutenberg University, 55099 Mainz, Germany}
L000091	\author{Ryusuke Jinno}
L000092	\email{jinno@phys.sci.kobe-u.ac.jp}
L000093	\affiliation{Department of Physics, Graduate School of Science, Kobe University, 1-1 Rokkodai, Kobe, Hyogo 657-8501, Japan}
L000094	
L000095	\begin{abstract}
L000096	
L000097	
L000098	
L000099	
L000100	
L000101	{Understanding whether primordial black holes form during strong first-order phase transition (FOPT) is a crucial open question in cosmology.} We address this using a fully covariant formalism to study cosmological perturbations, highlighting previously overlooked gauge dependencies. We show that non-covariant treatments can overestimate primordial black holes and scalar-induced gravitational waves. Once gauge dependencies are accounted for, both signals are strongly suppressed, {with direct implications for the FOPT interpretation of the Pulsar Timing Array signal.}
L000102	
L000103	
L000104	\vspace*{5pt} \noindent \textit{GitHub}: The {\tt deltaPT\,2.0} code to compute cosmological perturbations generated during strongly supercooled first-order phase transitions \href{https://github.com/YannGou/deltaPT2.0}{\faGithub\;YannGou/deltaPT\,2.0}.
L000105	
L000106	\vspace*{5pt}
L000107	\noindent
L000108	\textit{Published version:~\href{https://doi.org/10.1103/tfcx-kzqx}{Phys.Rev.Lett. 136 (2026) 17, 171404}}
L000109	\end{abstract}
L000110	
L000111	
L000112	
L000113	\preprint{CERN-TH-2025-044, KOBE-COSMO-25-05, MITP-25-016}
L000114	
L000115	\maketitle
L000116	
L000117	
L000118	\textit{\textbf{ Introduction.}}
L000119	{The Universe in the primordial epochs} is expected to possess a higher degree of symmetry than today~\cite{Kirzhnits:1972ut}.  As the Universe cools, these symmetries may be broken through first-order phase transitions (FOPTs), a generic feature of many early-Universe scenarios, during which bubbles of the new vacuum nucleate and expand, converting the old vacuum energy into heat and bulk fluid motion (see Ref.~\cite{Gouttenoire:2022gwi} for a review).
L000120	If the energy stored in the bubbles constitutes a large fraction of the total universe energy, {FOPTs have been shown to} leave sizable imprints on the spacetime metric, in terms of gravitational waves~\cite{Caprini:2015zlo,Caprini:2019egz}, primordial black holes (PBHs)~\cite{Kodama:1982sf,Hsu:1990fg,Liu:2021svg,Hashino:2021qoq,Jung:2021mku,Kawana:2022olo,Lewicki:2023ioy,Gouttenoire:2023naa,Baldes:2023rqv,Gouttenoire:2023bqy,Salvio:2023ynn,Gouttenoire:2023pxh,Jinno:2023vnr,Banerjee:2023qya,Flores:2024lng,Lewicki:2024ghw,Lewicki:2024sfw,Ai:2024cka,Conaci:2024tlc,Kanemura:2024pae,Banerjee:2024cwv,Hashino:2025fse,Murai:2025hse,Zou:2025sow,Ghoshal:2025dmi,Byrnes:2025tji}, and curvature perturbations~\cite{Sasaki:1982fi,Liu:2022lvz,Giombi:2023jqq,Elor:2023xbz,Lewicki:2024ghw,Buckley:2024nen,Cai:2024nln,Jinno:2024nwb}. {These curvature perturbations can source scalar-induced gravitational waves (SIGWs), studied in this context in~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw} and more generally in~\cite{Tomita:1975kj,Matarrese:1992rp,Matarrese:1993zf,Matarrese:1997ay,Acquaviva:2002ud,Mollerach:2003nq,Carbone:2004iv,Ananda:2006af,Baumann:2007zm,Espinosa:2018eve,Kohri:2018awv,Domenech:2021ztg}.}
L000121	
L000122	Since cosmological perturbations generally exhibit gauge dependence~\cite{Bardeen:1980kt}, reliable predictions for these late-time observables require a covariant treatment of perturbations and their evolution. {Such a treatment is required to assess whether FOPTs can produce observable PBHs and SIGWs, a question of central importance when evaluating the FOPT interpretation of the Pulsar Timing Array signal~\cite{Gouttenoire:2023bqy,Ellis:2023oxs,Lewicki:2024ghw}.}
L000123	
L000124	In this $\textit{letter}$, we derive the statistical properties of cosmological perturbations produced during a strong first-order phase transition, with a particular emphasis on the gauge choice and the calculation of gauge-invariant quantities.
L000125	We evaluate the abundance of PBHs and the spectrum of SIGWs using the most appropriate quantities for each: the density contrast in the comoving gauge $\delta^{(C)}$ for the former, to match the convention used to report the threshold for collapse in numerical simulations, while the gauge-invariant curvature perturbation $\mathcal{R}$ for the latter.
L000126	
L000127	\textit{\textbf{ Covariant linear perturbations from FOPTs.}} We model the nucleation rate by
L000128	\begin{equation}
L000129	\Gamma = H_n^4 \,e^{\beta (t-t_n)},
L000130	\end{equation}
L000131	where $H_n$ is the Hubble factor ($H\equiv \dot a(t) / a $, with $a$ being the scale factor) at $t=t_n$, defined as the nucleation time.
L000132	Adopting a relativistic bag equation of state, the energy and pressure density decompose as
L000133	\begin{equation}
L000134	\label{eq:rho_p_avg}
L000135	\rho=\rho_R+\rho_V,\qquad p=\rho_R/3-\rho_V,
L000136	\end{equation}
L000137	where $R$ and $V$ stand for radiation and vacuum components, respectively.
L000138	We consider the supercooled limit for which the latent heat $\Delta V$ is larger than the radiation energy density just before nucleation $\alpha \equiv \Delta V/\rho_{R} \gg 1$. At the background level, the radiation and vacuum energy density obeys the continuity equation
L000139	\begin{align}
L000140	\label{eq:bkg_continuity}
L000141	\overline{\rho_{\rm R}}'+4\mathcal{H}\overline{\rho_{\rm R}}=-\overline{\rho_{\rm V}}',\qquad \textrm{with}\quad \overline{\rho_{\rm V}} = \overline{F}\Delta V,
L000142	\end{align}
L000143	and the Friedmann equation $3M_{\rm pl}^2H^2=\overline{\rho_{\rm R}}+\overline{\rho_{\rm V}}$. The prime denotes the derivative with respect to the conformal time $\eta$ related to the cosmic time $t$ by $
L000144	\eta(t_2) =\eta(t_1) +\int^{t_2}_{t_1} d\tilde{t}/a(\tilde{t})$, and we defined $\mathcal{H}=aH$.
L000145	The remaining fraction of false vacuum $\overline{F}(t) $ is given by
L000146	\begin{equation}
L000147	\overline{F}(t) = \exp\left[-\frac{4\pi}{3}\!\! \int_{-\infty}^t \!\!\!\td t_n\, \Gamma(t_n) a(t_n)^3 \left(\int_{t_n}^t\!\! \frac{d\tilde{t}}{a(\tilde{t})}\right)^{\!3}\right],
L000148	\end{equation}
L000149	where we assumed bubble walls to expand at the speed of light $v_w \simeq 1$.
L000150	
L000151	Due to the stochastic nature of bubble nucleation, the stress-energy tensor $T^\mu_{\,\,\,\,\nu}$ acquires fluctuations, assumed to be statistically isotropic, $T^0_{\,\,\,\,0}=\overline{\rho}+\delta \rho$, $T^i_{\,\,\,\,j}=-(\overline{p}+\delta p)\delta^i_j+\Sigma^i_{~j}$, and $T^i_{\,\,\,\,0}=(\overline{\rho}+\overline{p})v^i$ where $v^i=dx^i/dt$ is the coordinate velocity. In the following, we use $\delta \equiv \delta \rho/\overline{\rho}$.
L000152	At the linear level, the 10 degrees of freedom of the symmetric $4\times4$ space-time metric $g_{\mu\nu}$ decomposes into 4 scalars, 4 vectors and 2 tensors. Keeping only the scalars ($A,B,C,E$), the spacetime metric reads
L000153	\begin{multline}
L000154	    \mathrm{d}s^2
L000155	    =
L000156	    a^2 \Bigl[
L000157	    \bigl(1 + 2A\bigr)\,\mathrm{d}\eta^2
L000158	    - 2\,\partial_i B\,\mathrm{d}x^i\,\mathrm{d}\eta  \\
L000159	-\big((1-2C)\delta_{ij}+2\partial_{\langle i}\partial_{j\rangle}E\big)dx^i dx^j\Bigr],
L000160	\end{multline}
L000161	where $\partial_{\langle i}\partial_{j\rangle} E \equiv (\partial_i\partial_j - \tfrac{1}{3}\delta_{ij}\nabla^2)E$. In the absence of anisotropic stress, scalar metric perturbations contain only one physical degree of freedom. The apparent redundancy in their description is known as ``gauge dependence''~\cite{Bardeen:1980kt}. To extract physical predictions, one must fix the coordinate freedom by choosing a gauge. The assumption of isotropy, $\Sigma^i_{~j} = 0$, imposes one constraint, leaving two additional scalar gauge degrees of freedom to fix.  A common choice is the spatially-flat gauge (F), defined by $C=E=0$, {in which we denote the density contrast by $\delta^{(F)}$.} Linearized Einstein equations in Fourier space lead to~\cite{SuppMat}
L000162	\color{black}
L000163	\begin{equation}
L000164	\label{eq:SF_continuity_prelude}
L000165	\tilde{\delta}^{(F)'}_\mathbf{k}+3\mathcal{H}(c_s^2-\omega)\tilde{\delta}^{(F)}_\mathbf{k}=(1+\omega) k^2\tilde{\mathcal{V}}_\mathbf{k}-3\mathcal{H}\tilde{\delta}_{p,\rm nad,\mathbf{k}},
L000166	\end{equation}
L000167	where $\tilde{X}_{\mathbf{k}}(t)$ denotes the Fourier-transform of $X(\mathbf{x},t)$.
L000168	The quantities $\omega \equiv \overline{p}/\overline{\rho}$ and $c_s^2\equiv {\overline{p}}'/{\overline{\rho}}'$ are the equation of state and speed of sound, respectively.
L000169	The quantity $\delta_{p,\rm nad}$ is the non-adiabatic pressure perturbation
L000170	\begin{align}
L000171	\delta_{p,\rm nad} \equiv \frac{\delta p_{\rm nad}}{\overline{\rho}},\quad \textrm{with}\quad \delta p_{\rm nad} \equiv \delta p^{(F)} - c_s^2 \delta \rho^{(F)},\label{eq:comoving_definition_deltapnad}
L000172	\end{align}
L000173	which is gauge-invariant in spite of the superscript $(F)$ on the right hand-side. Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:rho_p_avg}, Eq.~\eqref{eq:comoving_definition_deltapnad} becomes
L000174	\begin{equation}
L000175	\label{eq:delta_nad_FOPT_main}
L000176	\delta p_{\rm nad} = \frac{1-3c_s^2}{3}\overline{\rho}\,\delta^{(F)}-\frac{4}{3}\Delta V\delta F^{(F)},
L000177	\end{equation}
L000178	where $\delta F^{(F)} = F^{(F)}-\overline{F}$ with $F^{(F)}\equiv \rho_V^{(F)}/\Delta V$.
L000179	The quantity $\mathcal{V}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:SF_continuity_prelude} is the gauge-invariant scalar velocity $\mathcal{V}\equiv v+E'$ with $\partial_i(v)=\delta_{ij}v^j$. Einstein equation along $0i$ gives
L000180	\begin{equation}
L000181	\label{eq:GI_velocity}
L000182	\mathcal{V}= -\frac{2}{3(1+\omega)} \frac{\mathcal{H}\Phi+\Phi'}{\mathcal{H}^2},
L000183	\end{equation}
L000184	where $\Phi$ is the gauge-invariant Newtonian potential~\cite{Bardeen:1980kt}. A combination of Einstein equations along $00$ and $ij$ gives
L000185	\begin{equation}
L000186	\label{eq:Newtonian_potential}
L000187	\tilde{\Phi}_{\mathbf{k}}'' + 3 (1 + c_s^2)\mathcal{H} \tilde{\Phi}_{\mathbf{k}}'
L000188	+[3 (c_s^2-\omega)\mathcal{H}^2+c_s^2 k^2]\tilde{\Phi}_{\mathbf{k}}
L000189	= \frac{3}{2}\mathcal{H}^2\tilde{\delta}_{p,\rm nad,\mathbf{k}}.
L000190	\end{equation}
L000191	The initial conditions for the system Eqs.~\eqref{eq:SF_continuity_prelude} and \eqref{eq:Newtonian_potential} are
L000192	\begin{equation}
L000193	\tilde{\delta}^{(F)}_{\mathbf{k}}(0)=0,\qquad \tilde{\Phi}_{\mathbf{k}}(0)=0,\qquad \tilde{\Phi}_{\mathbf{k}}'(0)=0,
L000194	\end{equation}
L000195	where we have neglected the primordial curvature perturbations (i.e.~generated during inflation), which we assume to be small at these scales.
L000196	For more details on cosmological perturbation theory, we refer the reader to the Supplemental Material (SuM) or Refs.~\cite{Kodama:1984ziu,Mukhanov:1990me,Mukhanov:2005sc,Baumann:2022mni,Peter:2013avv}.
L000197	
L000198	In the spatially-flat gauge, constant-time hypersurfaces are unperturbed
L000199	$ds^2\big|_{\eta={\rm const.}}=-a^2dx^2$.
L000200	This is particularly convenient since by plugging Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_nad_FOPT_main} into Eq.~\eqref{eq:SF_continuity_prelude}, we get a perturbation equation
L000201	\begin{align}
L000202	\delta{\tilde{\rho}}_{R,{\mathbf{k}}}^{(F)'}+4\mathcal{H}\delta{\tilde{\rho}}_{R,{\mathbf{k}}}^{(F)}=-\delta{\tilde{\rho}}_{\rm V,{\mathbf{k}}}^{(F)'}+\frac{4}{3}\overline{\rho_R}\, k^2 \tilde{\mathcal{V}}_{\mathbf{k}},\label{eq:SF_continuity}
L000203	\end{align}
L000204	which coincides with the background equation in Eq.~\eqref{eq:bkg_continuity} in the super-Hubble limit $k\ll \mathcal{H}$.
L000205	Hence, in the spatially-flat gauge and in the super-Hubble limit, different patches evolve as distinct FLRW universes \cite{Wands:2000dp},
L000206	and we can identify
L000207	\begin{equation}
L000208	\label{eq:F_k_F}
L000209	\tilde{F}^{(F)}_{\mathbf{k}} \underset{k\ll\mathcal{H}}{\simeq} \tilde{F}^{\rm (FLRW)}_{\mathbf{k}},
L000210	\end{equation}
L000211	where $F^{\rm (FLRW)}$ is the false vacuum fraction in a flat FLRW universe. Notice that such equality does not hold in other gauges. The code \deltaPT~developed in Ref.~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw} calculates the false vacuum fraction $F^{\rm (FLRW)}_{\rm avg}(R,t)$ averaged over a ball of radius $R$, in a flat FLRW universe. In this work, we rely on the approximation~\footnote{We leave a proper de-convolution from the top-hat window function $W(r,R)=\Theta(R-r)/V$ followed by inverse-Fourier transformation for future work.
L000212	}
L000213	\begin{equation}
L000214	\label{eq:F_k_F_avg}
L000215	\tilde{F}^{\rm (FLRW)}_{\mathbf{k}}(t) \simeq V F^{\rm (FLRW)}_{\rm avg}(R=k^{-1},t),
L000216	\end{equation}
L000217	with $V=4\pi R^3/3$.
L000218	The factor $V$ ensures that Fourier transforms carry the usual volume dimension.
L000219	Due to Eq.~\eqref{eq:F_k_F_avg}, it follows that $\tilde{X}_{\mathbf{k}}(t)\simeq V X_{\rm avg}(R=k^{-1},t)$ for any perturbations $X$ derived in this work.
L000220	This approximation should become exact in the super-Hubble limit $k\ll \mathcal{H}$ when gradient terms are negligible. We now denote perturbations by $X\equiv \tilde{X}_{\mathbf{k}}/V\simeq X_{\rm avg}(R=k^{-1})$.
L000221	
L000222	\begin{figure}[t]
L000223	\centering
L000224	\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{perturbations_traj_letter}
L000225	\caption{\label{fig:delta_Phi_numerical}
L000226	Time evolution of the comoving curvature perturbation $\mathcal{R}$, and the density contrast in the spatially-flat $\delta^{(F)}$ and comoving $\delta^{(C)}$ gauge during a FOPT. We show perturbations $X$ averaged over a comoving volume $4\pi k^{-3}/3$.
L000227	We also report the non-adiabatic pressure $\delta p_{\rm nad}$ peaking close to Hubble crossing epoch $\mathcal{H}=k$. For presentation purposes we assume $\beta / H = 4$ and show the wavenumber $k = 0.9 k_{\rm max}$, where we identify $k_{\rm max}$ as the maximal ${\cal H}$. $j=1$ indicates the time of first bubble nucleation, which is slightly delayed with respect to average (orange vs black dashed line).}
L000228	\end{figure}
L000229	
L000230	In practice, the evolution of perturbations can be derived as follows: from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:F_k_F}, \eqref{eq:F_k_F_avg} and \deltaPT, we calculate $\delta p_{\rm nad}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_nad_FOPT_main}, then we solve the closed system of equations in \eqref{eq:SF_continuity_prelude} and \eqref{eq:Newtonian_potential} to determine $\Phi$ and $\delta^{(F)}$, see SuM for more details. Another common choice of coordinate is the comoving gauge (C), in which the fluid velocity perturbation vanishes ($v + B = 0,~ E=0$).
L000231	The density contrast in the comoving gauge can be derived using the transformation rules
L000232	\begin{align}
L000233	&\delta^{(C)}=\delta^{(F)}+ 3(1+\omega)\mathcal{R},\label{eq:delta_C_F}
L000234	\end{align}
L000235	where $\mathcal{R}$ is the comoving curvature perturbation given by the gauge-invariant expression~\cite{Bardeen:1980kt}
L000236	\begin{align}
L000237	\mathcal{R} ~\equiv ~\Phi - \mathcal{H}\mathcal{V} ~=~ \frac{5+3\omega}{3+3\omega} \Phi +\frac{2\Phi'}{3(1+\omega)\mathcal{H}}.
L000238	\label{eq:mathcalR_def_main}
L000239	\end{align}
L000240	{The transformation rule in Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_C_F}  follows from the textbook relationship $\zeta =\mathcal{R}-\delta^{(C)}/3(1+\omega)$~\cite{Baumann:2022mni} where $\zeta~\equiv~ -\delta^{(F)}/3(1+\omega)$ is the curvature perturbation on uniform-density hypersurfaces, see SuM for details.}  {Building on this framework, our public GitHub release \deltaPTvTwo~\cite{deltaPTv2} extends \deltaPT~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw} to compute the covariant cosmological perturbations analyzed in this work.}
L000241	We show the time evolution of $\delta^{(F)}$, $\delta^{(C)}$, $\mathcal{R}$ and $\delta p_{\rm nad}$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:delta_Phi_numerical}, for a single realization of the bubble nucleation dynamics within a ball of radius $R=k^{-1}$ operated with \deltaPTvTwo~\cite{deltaPTv2} . {We derive the perturbations statistics by making $N_{\rm sim}=10^6$ realizations of random bubble nucleation histories and storing the Hubble-crossing values of the perturbations in a histogram~\footnote{{In each patch, we randomly sample the nucleation times $t_j$ and positions $d_j$ of the first $j_c=50$ bubbles and assume a deterministic evolution for subsequent nucleation dynamics~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}. The probability distributions for $t_j$ and $d_j$ within a patch of radius $R$ are derived analytically in App.~A of Ref.~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw} and are summarized in the SuM.}
L000242	}.}
L000243	In Fig.~\ref{fig:proba_distrib_P_deltaC}, we show the resulting probability density function (PDF) of the density contrast. The variance of the density contrast in the spatially-flat and comoving gauges, as well as the comoving curvature perturbation are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:PS_allquantities}.
L000244	\begin{figure}[ht!]
L000245	\centering
L000246	\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{PDF_deltaC_vs_deltaF}
L000247	\caption{\label{fig:proba_distrib_P_deltaC}
L000248	Probability distribution of the density contrast in the comoving (blue) and spatially-flat (red) gauges, obtained from {solving cosmological perturbations equations for $N_{\rm sim}=10^6$ distinct random nucleation histories inside a patch of comoving volume $4\pi k^{-3}/3$.} The dashed lines show the best fits using the distribution in Eq.~\eqref{eq:fit_function}. The dotted lines shows the best fits using a Gaussian distribution.
L000249	We choose $k=0.9k_{\rm max}$ where $k_{\rm max}$ is the scale entering at percolation.
L000250	The colored boxes indicate the collapse condition in the comoving (blue) and spatially-flat (red) gauges. We cut the box from below at the indicative amplitude for a stellar mass PBH. }
L000251	\end{figure}
L000252	
L000253	\begin{comment}
L000254	Neglecting the time derivative corrections $\Phi'\simeq 0$ in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:delta_C_F}, \eqref{eq:delta_N_F}, \eqref{eq:mathcalR_def_main}, we obtain over super-Hubble scales $k\ll \mathcal{H}$
L000255	\begin{equation}
L000256	\label{eq:R_delta_relationship}
L000257	-\mathcal{R}~ \simeq ~ \frac{\delta^{(F)}}{4}~\simeq~ \frac{3}{4}\delta^{(N)}~ \simeq ~ \frac{9}{4}\left(\frac{k}{\mathcal{H}}\right)^2\delta^{(C)}.
L000258	\end{equation}
L000259	\end{comment}
L000260	
L000261	
L000262	\textit{\textbf{ Primordial Black Hole formation.}}
L000263	Once the density contrast in a Hubble patch exceeds a threshold, it collapses into a PBH~\cite{Zeldovich:1967lct,Hawking:1971ei,Carr:1974nx,Carr:1975qj,Chapline:1975ojl}. Numerical relativity simulations~\cite{Escriva:2021aeh} define the collapse threshold in terms of the compaction function evaluated at Hubble crossing $t_H$~\cite{Shibata:1999zs,Harada:2023ffo}. At this epoch, it reduces to the density contrast averaged over a Hubble-scale region, $\delta_{\rm avg}(R=k^{-1},t_H)$~\cite{Musco:2018rwt,Escriva:2019phb}, which in our notation corresponds to $\delta(k\simeq\mathcal{H})$.
L000264	As we have seen, the latter is gauge-dependent, see also Ref.~\cite{Harada:2015yda}. Recent simulations have relied on the comoving gauge, see e.g.~\cite{Musco:2018rwt}. This gauge is advantageous since the $00$ component of Einstein's equations reduces to Poisson’s equation
L000265	\begin{equation}
L000266	\label{eq:Poisson_eq}
L000267	    \Delta \Phi = \frac{3}{2}\mathcal{H}^2\delta^{(C)}.
L000268	\end{equation}
L000269	 In this gauge, a patch collapses into a PBH if~\cite{Musco:2018rwt,Escriva:2019phb,Escriva:2021aeh}
L000270	\begin{equation}
L000271	\label{eq:PBH_threshold}
L000272	\delta^{(C)}(k\simeq\mathcal{H}) \gtrsim \delta_c^{(C)} \in [0.40,\,0.67],
L000273	\end{equation}
L000274	with the exact value depending on the curvature {real space} profile \cite{Musco:2020jjb}. These simulations assume adiabatic evolution from super-Hubble scales~\cite{Musco:2018rwt,Escriva:2019phb,Escriva:2021aeh}. In our scenario, {non-adiabatic effects are present} but decay rapidly after percolation (see Fig.~\ref{fig:delta_Phi_numerical}), and we therefore adopt the standard collapse criterion evaluated at $k=\mathcal{H}$.
L000275	
L000276	
L000277	
L000278	We proceed to estimate the abundance of primordial black holes. Denoting $P(\delta^{(C)})$ as the PDF for $\delta^{(C)}$ at $k={\cal H}$, the PBH mass fraction at formation reads
L000279	\begin{align}
L000280	\label{eq:beta_k_M_1stOPT}
L000281	\beta_k(M) &= \int_{\delta^{(C)}_c}^{\infty}
L000282	{\rm d}\delta^{(C)} \,
L000283	\left (\frac{M(\delta^{(C)})}{M_k} \right )
L000284	P(\delta^{(C)}) .
L000285	\end{align}
L000286	In the first line of Eq.~\eqref{eq:beta_k_M_1stOPT}, the PBH mass $M$ is related to $\delta^{(C)}$ through the critical scaling law \cite{Choptuik:1992jv,Evans:1994pj,Niemeyer:1997mt,Musco:2012au}
L000287	\begin{equation}
L000288	\label{eq:choptuik_law}
L000289	M(\delta^{(C)}) = {\cal K} M_k (\delta^{(C)} - \delta_c^{(C)})^\gamma,
L000290	\end{equation}
L000291	with $\gamma = 0.36$, ${\cal K} \sim 3$, and
L000292	$M_k = 4\pi M_{\rm pl}^2/H_k$
L000293	represents the mass within the Hubble sphere when scale $k$ re-enters, $H_k=k/a$.
L000294	Using that PBHs redshift like matter, we obtain the DM abundance composed of PBH $f_{\rm PBH}$
L000295	\begin{equation}
L000296	\frac{df_{\rm PBH}}{d\ln(M)} \simeq \int d\ln{k}\,\left(\frac{\beta_k(M)}{3 \times 10^{-10}}\right)\left(\frac{T_k}{\rm GeV}\right),
L000297	\end{equation}
L000298	where $T_k$ is the temperature at Hubble crossing.
L000299	Approximating the spectrum to a nearly monochromatic distribution close to $k \sim k_{\rm max}$, one parametrically finds
L000300	\begin{equation}
L000301	f_{\rm PBH} ~\sim~
L000302	\left (\frac{P(\delta_c^{(C)})}{10^{-10}}\right )\,
L000303	\left (\frac{T_{k}}{\rm GeV} \right ),
L000304	\end{equation}
L000305	where the Hubble crossing temperature is related to the Hubble mass as
L000306	\begin{equation}
L000307	M_k(T_k)
L000308	= 4.8\times 10^{-2} M_\odot
L000309	\left(\frac{106.75}{g_*} \right)^{1/2} \left( \frac{\rm GeV}{T_k} \right)^{2},
L000310	\end{equation}
L000311	and $g_*$ is the number of relativistic degrees of freedom.
L000312	
L000313	As shown in blue in Fig.~\ref{fig:proba_distrib_P_deltaC}, the distribution
L000314	$P(\delta^{(C)})$, here plotted for $k=0.9k_{\rm max}$, is negatively skewed with an extended tail in the negative range and an exponential suppression of large overdensities, reflecting the exponentially-low probability of forming late-blooming patches~\cite{Gouttenoire:2023naa}. As in Refs.~\cite{Tomberg:2023kli,Lewicki:2024ghw}, the fit (with $\epsilon, \sigma > 0$)
L000315	\begin{equation}
L000316	\label{eq:fit_function}
L000317	P(\delta) \propto \exp\!\left[\frac{\epsilon}{2}(\delta-\mu) - \frac{2}{\epsilon^2\sigma^2}\left(1 - e^{\frac{\epsilon}{2}(\delta-\mu)}\right)^2\right]
L000318	\end{equation}
L000319	accurately describes the data (see SuM).
L000320	
L000321	Due to the negative non-Gaussianity (NG), even for a completion rate as low as $\beta/H=4$,
L000322	$P({\delta^{(C)}})$ drops sharply around $\delta^{(C)}\sim 0.1$ and is strongly suppressed above the optimistic PBH threshold $\delta^{(C)}_c\gtrsim 0.4$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:PBH_threshold}. We found similar results for $\beta/H =2$, just above the no-completion boundary. We conclude that within the framework considered here, slow FOPTs do not appear to yield PBHs in observable amounts. The PDF for $\delta^{(F)}$ in the spatially-flat gauge, shown in red in Fig.~\ref{fig:proba_distrib_P_deltaC}, is broader but still heavily skewed, and with the collapse threshold shifted to{
L000323	\begin{equation}
L000324	\label{eq:delta_F_delta_C_link}
L000325	    \delta^{(F)}_c\simeq 10\,\delta^{(C)}_c
L000326	\end{equation}
L000327	following from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:delta_C_F} and \eqref{eq:Poisson_eq} with $k=\mathcal{H}$, $\omega=1/3$, and $\Phi'\simeq 0$~\footnote{As known from textbooks, e.g.~\cite{Baumann:2022mni}, and re-derived in the SuM, the approximation $\Phi'\simeq0$ holds for superhorizon modes only in the absence of non-adiabatic pressure perturbations, $\delta_{p,\mathrm{nad}}=0$. In our scenario, bubble dynamics generate $\delta_{p,\mathrm{nad}}\neq0$, leading to departures from $\Phi'\simeq0$ on superhorizon scales. Nevertheless, $\Phi'\simeq0$ remains a good approximation near horizon crossing, $k\simeq \mathcal{H}$, justifying its use in evaluating the PBH collapse threshold. The time evolution of $\Phi(t)$ is shown in the SuM.
L000328	},} the conclusion remains unchanged. Note that our analysis neglects non-linear corrections to the density-curvature relation, which would slightly reduce the variance of $\delta^{(C)}$ further \cite{DeLuca:2019qsy,Young:2019yug}.
L000329	
L000330	\begin{figure}[ht]
L000331	\centering
L000332	\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{variance_betaOH4_7_minimal}
L000333	\caption{\label{fig:PS_allquantities}
L000334	Variance of the perturbations $X$ averaged over a ball of comoving radius $k^{-1}$, $\sigma_{X}^2\equiv \left< X_{\rm avg}^2(R=k^{-1},t_H)\right>$, evaluated at Hubble crossing epoch $t_H$. We show the comoving curvature perturbation $\mathcal{R}$ (red), the density contrast in the spatially-flat $\delta^{(F)}$ (blue) and comoving gauge $\delta^{(C)}$ (yellow), and the non-adiabatic pressure perturbation $\delta_{p,{\rm nad}}$ (green).
L000335	}
L000336	\end{figure}
L000337	
L000338	\textit{\textbf{ Induced Gravitational Waves.}} We now focus on the GW spectrum from FOPT, seizing the relevance of SIGWs from curvature perturbations.
L000339	The GW abundance today can be written as
L000340	\begin{equation}
L000341	\Omega_{\rm GW}(k)=\frac{1.7\times10^{-5}}{g_*^{1/3}(T_k)}\Big[\Omega_{\rm PGW}(k,T_k)+\Omega_{\rm SIGW}(k,T_k)\Big],
L000342	\end{equation}
L000343	where $\Omega_{\rm PGW}$ and $\Omega_{\rm SIGW}$ are the spectrum of primary GWs (PGWs) and SIGWs.
L000344	Primary GW are produced from bubble collision and relativistic shells. Assuming that the latter remain thin and conserve their energy after collision, their GW spectrum is approximated by the broken power-law bulk flow formula~\cite{Jinno:2017fby,Konstandin:2017sat,Baldes:2024wuz}
L000345	\begin{equation}
L000346	\Omega_{\rm PGW} \simeq 0.06 \left( \frac{H}{\beta}\right)^2
L000347	\frac{(a+b) f^a f_\star^b}{\left(af^{a+b}+b f_\star^{a+b} \right)}S_H(f),
L000348	\end{equation}
L000349	with $f_\star=0.8(\beta/H)(\mathcal{H}/2\pi)$, $a=0.9$, $b=2.1$, and $S_H(f)$ imposing a $f^3$ behavior for $f<\mathcal{H}/2\pi$.
L000350	SIGWs are given by~\cite{Tomita:1975kj,Matarrese:1992rp,Matarrese:1993zf,Matarrese:1997ay,Acquaviva:2002ud,Mollerach:2003nq,Carbone:2004iv,Ananda:2006af,Baumann:2007zm,Domenech:2021ztg,Espinosa:2018eve,Kohri:2018awv}
L000351	\begin{equation}
L000352	\Omega_{\rm SIGW}(k,\eta)= \frac{1}{24}\left(\frac{k}{{\cal H}(\eta)}\right)^2\overline{{\cal P}_h(k,\eta)},
L000353	\end{equation}
L000354	where the power spectrum induced at second-order from curvature perturbations is
L000355	\begin{align}
L000356	\overline{ {\cal P}_h(k,\eta)} &
L000357	(2\pi)^3\delta_D^{(3)}(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{k}')
L000358	= \frac{16 k^3}{2 \pi^2}
L000359	\sum_s
L000360	\int\frac{{\rm d}^3\mathbf{p}}{(2\pi)^{3}}
L000361	\int\frac{{\rm d}^3\mathbf{q}}{(2\pi)^{3}}
L000362	\nonumber \\
L000363	&
L000364	\times
L000365	\overline{J_s(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{p},\eta)
L000366	J_s(\mathbf{k}',\mathbf{q},\eta) }
L000367	\langle
L000368	\tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{\mathbf{p}}
L000369	\tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{p}}
L000370	\tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{\mathbf{q}}
L000371	\tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{\mathbf{k'}-\mathbf{q}}
L000372	\rangle\,,
L000373	\label{eq:Phfull}
L000374	\end{align}
L000375	having defined
L000376	\begin{equation}
L000377	J_s(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{p},\eta)
L000378	\equiv Q_{s}(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{p})
L000379	I(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{p},\eta)
L000380	\end{equation}
L000381	\begin{figure}[ht!]
L000382	\centering
L000383	\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{2ndGW_withNG.pdf}
L000384	\caption{\label{fig:2ndGW}
L000385	SIGW contribution to the GWs from FOPT. The solid line reports the total $\Omega_{\rm GW}$, while the dotted line the subdominant SIGWs, which includes the (negligible) NG corrections.
L000386	}
L000387	\end{figure}
L000388	as the product of the polarization tensor contracted with the loop momenta $Q_{s}(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{p}) = e_{s}^{ij}(\hat{\mathbf{k}})p_ip_j$, and the kernel function $ I(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{p},\eta)$ being the convolution of source and Green function, see e.g.~Ref.~\cite{LISACosmologyWorkingGroup:2025vdz} and references therein for the explicit expressions.
L000389	The overline denotes a time average over oscillations~\cite{Maggiore:1999vm} and $s$ labels the two GW polarizations. We take the Gaussian limit in which only disconnected contractions contribute to the four-point function in \eqref{eq:Phfull}.
L000390	We extract the curvature power spectrum,
L000391	$\langle \tilde{\mathcal{R}}(\mathbf{k})\tilde{\mathcal{R}}(\mathbf{k}')\rangle = (2\pi)^3\delta_D^{(3)}(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{k}')(2\pi^2/k^3)\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k)$, from the variance of the volume-averaged curvature perturbation $\sigma_{\mathcal{R}}^2$ shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:PS_allquantities} by inverting the formula
L000392	\begin{align}
L000393	\label{eq:variance_power_spectrum}
L000394	\sigma_{\mathcal{R}}^2\equiv \left< \mathcal{R}_{\rm avg}^2\right>
L000395	= \int \frac{{\rm d}k}{k}\,| \tilde{W}(k,R)|^2 \mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k),
L000396	\end{align}
L000397	where $\tilde{W}(k,R)$ is the Fourier-transform of the top-hat window function. Using $\mathcal{P}_\mathcal{R}(k)\propto k^3$ we obtain $\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k=R^{-1}) \simeq 2\sigma_\mathcal{R}^2/3\pi$.
L000398	Fig.~\ref{fig:2ndGW} shows the resulting GW spectra for various $\beta/H$.
L000399	We conclude that SIGWs are never strong enough to form a secondary peak in the total spectrum and at most affect the far-IR tail due to the logarithmic scaling
L000400	$\Omega_{\rm SIGW} (k \ll k_{\star}) \, {\propto}\, k^3(1 + \tilde A \ln^2(k/\tilde k))$~\cite{Atal:2021jyo}. {In the SuM, we show that non-Gaussianities, quantified by the skewness $\langle\mathcal{R}^3\rangle$~\cite{Luo:1992er,Komatsu:2001rj,Nakama:2016gzw,Garcia-Bellido:2017aan,Unal:2018yaa,Cai:2018dig,Cai:2019amo,Ragavendra:2020sop,Yuan:2020iwf,Adshead:2021hnm,Davies:2021loj,Abe:2022xur,Garcia-Saenz:2022tzu,Garcia-Saenz:2023zue,Li:2023xtl,Yuan:2023ofl,Perna:2024ehx,Gouttenoire:2025jxe}, have negligible contributions to the SIGW signal.}
L000401	
L000402	
L000403	
L000404	{\textit{\textbf{ Discussion and implications of our results.}}}
L000405	In this work we have employed a covariant formalism to study cosmological perturbations originating from a FOPT, with implications for PBHs and SIGWs.
L000406	Upon modeling bubble dynamics on a FLRW background, previous works have implicitly chosen the spatially-flat gauge (F). However, the values of the density contrast at the PBH formation threshold $\delta^{(C)}_c\in[0.4,0.67]$ and its relation to the comoving curvature perturbation at Hubble crossing $\mathcal{R}\simeq -9\delta^{(C)}/4 $~\footnote{{The relation $\mathcal{R}\simeq -9\delta^{(C)}/4$ follows from Eqs.~\eqref{eq:mathcalR_def_main} and \eqref{eq:Poisson_eq} for $k=\mathcal{H}$, $\omega=1/3$, and $\Phi'\simeq 0$.}} -- widely quoted in the PBH literature -- are valid in the comoving gauge (C).
L000407	At Hubble crossing, the density contrast in the spatially-flat and comoving gauges are related by $\delta^{(F)} \simeq 10\delta^{(C)}$, {see Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_F_delta_C_link}.}
L000408	Misidentifying these two gauges leads to an underestimation of the PBH collapse threshold by a factor of 10, leading to a dramatic overestimation of the PBH abundance.
L000409	The same gauge confusion overestimates the comoving curvature perturbation $\mathcal{R}$ by a factor $10$ and therefore overestimates SIGWs by a factor of $10^4$.
L000410	Additionally, we identified a factor of $2/3\pi$ between the variance of the spatially averaged $\mathcal{R}$ and its power spectrum, {see below Eq.~\eqref{eq:variance_power_spectrum},} further suppressing SIGWs by a factor of $20$, {leading to lower SIGWs amplitude with respect to Ref.~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw} by a factor $2\times 10^5$.}
L000411	
L000412	{Let us now discuss the implication of the fact that PBH and SIGW production from FOPTs appear to be far smaller than previously thought. First, this implies it would be harder to use PBHs to search for evidence of strong FOPTs having taken place in the primordial universe.
L000413	Secondly, this result has important implications for the FOPT interpretation of the PTA signal: the loudest region of the posterior is not necessarily excluded by PBH overproduction constraints, particularly those from LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA~\cite{Gouttenoire:2023bqy,Ellis:2023oxs,Lewicki:2024ghw}, and FOPTs with a reheating temperature above a GeV might not explain the PTA signal~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}.
L000414	}
L000415	
L000416	{\textit{\textbf{Limitations and Future Directions}}}
L000417	We have neglected gravitational effects on nucleation rates~\cite{Coleman:1980aw} and on the expansion of bubbles~\cite{Giombi:2023jqq,Jinno:2024nwb}, as well as the gradient energy of bubble walls~\cite{Lewicki:2023ioy,Flores:2024lng,Hashino:2025fse}.
L000418	The actual distribution of radiation converted from the vacuum energy is also important in understanding perturbations on small scales $k \gtrsim \mathcal{H}$~\cite{Jinno:2019jhi,Lewicki:2022pdb}.
L000419	{While asphericities have a small impact on the threshold for PBH collapse in the standard scenario \cite{Yoo:2020lmg}, it is not yet clear whether the same conclusion applies in the present case.}
L000420	Future work should incorporate these effects and perform full numerical simulations tracking non-linear curvature perturbations and non-adiabatic pressure effects to refine PBH formation criteria.
