L000831	
L000832	\section{Application to a First-Order Phase Transition}
L000833	\label{app:Bubble_growth_theory}
L000834	
L000835	In the previous section, we reviewed the theory of cosmological perturbations. This framework can be applied to any specific scenario once the relevant input quantities are specified, namely the equation-of-state parameter $\omega$, the sound speed $c_s^2$, the non-adiabatic pressure perturbation $\delta p_{\rm nad}$, and the initial conditions.
L000836	We now apply this formalism to a first-order phase transition. In Sec.~\ref{subsec:deltapnad}, we derive the expressions for the aforementioned input quantities. The non-adiabatic pressure perturbation $\delta p_{\rm nad}$ relies on the false vacuum fraction $F$ which we show how to compute with $\deltaPT$ in Sec.~\ref{subsec:falsevacuum}. Finally, the cosmological perturbations are numerically calculated in Sec.~\ref{subsec:cosmo_pert}.
L000837	
L000838	
L000839	\subsection{The non-adiabatic pressure fluctuation}
L000840	\label{subsec:deltapnad}
L000841	
L000842	During a FOPT, the energy and pressure densities are
L000843	\begin{equation}
L000844	\label{eq:rho_p_bar_app}
L000845	\rho=\rho_R+\rho_V,\qquad \textrm{and}\qquad p=\rho_R/3-\rho_V,\qquad \textrm{with}\qquad \rho_V(t) = \Delta V F(t),
L000846	\end{equation}
L000847	where $\Delta V$ is the vacuum energy difference accross the phase transition and $F$ is the volume fraction still remaining in the old vacuum.
L000848	We deduce the equation of state $\omega=\overline{p}/\overline{\rho}$ and speed of sound $c_s^2=\overline{p}'/\overline{\rho}'$  of the universe
L000849	\begin{equation}
L000850	\label{eq:omega_cs_app}
L000851	\omega = \frac{\overline{\rho}_R/3-\overline{\rho_V}}{\overline{\rho_R}+\overline{\rho_V}},\qquad \textrm{and}\qquad  c_s^2 = \frac{1}{3}\left(1+\frac{\overline{\rho_V}'}{\mathcal{H}\overline{\rho_{R}}} \right).
L000852	\end{equation}
L000853	Neglecting prior seeds like primordial curvature perturbations produced during inflation and responsible for galaxy formation, the growth of the linear perturbations --- whether it is the gravitational potential $\Phi$, the density contrast $\delta^{(X)}$ in a given gauge $(X)$ or the gauge-invariant velocity $\mathcal{V}$ --- are sourced by the non-adiabatic perturbation $\delta p_{\rm nad} =\delta p^{(X)}-c_s^2\delta\rho^{(X)}$. Using Eqs.~\eqref{eq:rho_p_bar_app} and \eqref{eq:omega_cs_app}, we can write
L000854	\begin{equation}
L000855	\label{eq:delta_nad_FOPT}
L000856	\delta p_{\rm nad} = \frac{1-3c_s^2}{3}\overline{\rho}\,\delta^{(X)}-\frac{4}{3}\Delta V(F_k^{(X)}-\overline{F}),
L000857	\end{equation}
L000858	where $F_k^{(X)}=\rho_{\rm V}^{(X)}/\Delta V$ is the false vacuum fraction in a finite comoving volume $V(k)=4\pi k^{-3}/3$ and $\overline{F}$ is the background value.
L000859	The spatially-flat gauge (F) is particularly convenient,
L000860	since constant-time hypersurfaces are flat, cf.~Eq.~\eqref{eq:SF_gauge}. As discussed in the main text, this suggests that the false
L000861	vacuum fraction in the spatially-flat gauge can be modeled by assuming an FLRW
L000862	background, which we adopt here as a working approximation,
L000863	\begin{equation}
L000864	\label{eq:F_k_F_k_FLRW}
L000865	F_k^{(F)} \simeq F_k^{(\mathrm{FLRW})},
L000866	\end{equation}
L000867	where $F_k^{(\mathrm{FLRW})}$ is evaluated using \deltaPT~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}.
L000868	Note that the expression for $F_k^{(X)}$ in the other gauges can determined by recalling that the non-adiabatic pressure perturbation $\delta p_{\rm nad}$ is gauge-independent, see e.g. Eq.~\eqref{eq:R_prime_Phi_prime}. In order to leave Eq.~\eqref{eq:delta_nad_FOPT} invariant under a gauge transformation $X\to Y$, the false vacuum fraction $F_k^{(X)}$ must transform as
L000869	\begin{equation}
L000870	F_k^{(X)}- F_k^{(Y)} = \frac{1-3c_s^2}{4}\frac{\overline{\rho}}{\Delta V}\left(\delta^{(X)}-\delta^{(Y)}\right).
L000871	\end{equation}
L000872	
L000873	
L000874	
L000875	\subsection{The false vacuum fraction}
L000876	\label{subsec:falsevacuum}
L000877	
L000878	The false vacuum fraction in a finite comoving volume $V(k)=4\pi k^{-3}/3$ in a FLRW background $F_k^{(\rm FLRW)}$, introduced in Eq.~\eqref{eq:F_k_F_k_FLRW}, can be calculated with the public code \deltaPT~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}{, now upgraded to \deltaPTvTwo~\cite{deltaPTv2}.}
L000879	We now review the calculation.
L000880	The false vacuum fraction is decomposed into two components: one that arises from the first $j_c$ bubbles and another from the remaining bubbles~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}
L000881	\begin{equation}
L000882	\label{eq:F_FLRW_def_app}
L000883	F_k^{(\rm FLRW)}(t) = F_k^{(j\leq j_c)}(t)F_k^{(j> j_c)}(t).
L000884	\end{equation}
L000885	The contribution from the first $j_c$ bubbles is computed stochastically by drawing,
L000886	from their respective probability distributions, the times $\{t_j\}_{j\le j_c}$ at
L000887	which the $j^{\rm th}$ bubble first nucleates inside or reaches the volume $V(k)$,
L000888	together with the corresponding distances $\{d_j\}_{j\le j_c}$ from the center of
L000889	$V(k)$. The probability distribution for $t_j$ is a Poisson distribution~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}
L000890	\begin{equation} \label{eq:poisson}
L000891	p_{t,j}(t;k) = \frac{\td \bar{N}_k(t)}{\td t} \frac{\bar{N}_k(t)^{j-1}}{\Gamma(j)} e^{-\bar{N}_k(t)} ,\qquad \textrm{with}\quad \bar{N}_k(t) = \frac{4\pi}{3} \int_{-\infty}^t \td t_n, \Gamma(t_n) a(t_n)^3 \left[ k^{-1} + R(t;t_n) \right]^3.
L000892	\end{equation}
L000893	The quantity $\bar{N}_k(t)$ is the average number of bubbles with non-vanishing overlap with the volume $a^3 V(k)$ at time $t$. We recall that $\Gamma(t)=H_0^4\,\textrm{exp}(\beta t)$ is the nucleation rate and $H_0$ the Hubble rate in $t=0$.
L000894	A bubble that reaches or nucleates within the volume $V(k)$ at time $t_j$ must have formed at a distance $d_j < k^{-1} + R(t;t_n)$, where $t_n$ is the time of nucleation. The probability distribution for this distance, $d_j$, increases with $d_j^2$ up to the maximum radius~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}
L000895	\begin{equation} \label{eq:pd}
L000896	p_d(d_j;t,k) = \frac{4\pi d_j^2}{\bar{N}_k(t)} \int_{-\infty}^t \td t_n \Gamma(t_n) a(t_n)^3 \theta(k^{-1} + R(t;t_n) - d_j) .
L000897	\end{equation}
L000898	The contribution of the first $j_c$ bubbles is~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}
L000899	\begin{equation} \label{eq:Fk_first}
L000900	F_k^{(j\leq j_c)}(t) \simeq \prod_{j=1}^{j_c} \left[1-f(t;t_j,d_j,k)\right] ,\qquad \textrm{with} \quad f(t;t_j,d_j,k) = \frac{V_{\rm int}(d_j,\max[0,d_j-k^{-1}]+R(t;t_j),k^{-1})}{V(k)}.
L000901	\end{equation}
L000902	The quantity $f(t;t_j,d_j,k)$ is the fraction of the volume $V(k)$ occupied by the $j$th bubble.
L000903	The quantity $V_{\rm int}$ is the volume of the overlap between two spheres, with radii $R$ and $r$ and separated by a distance $d$~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}
L000904	\begin{equation}
L000905	V_{\rm int}(d,R,r) =
L000906	\begin{cases}
L000907	\frac{4\pi}{3} d^3 \xi\left(\frac{R}{d},\frac{r}{d}\right) & \text{if } |R-r| \leq d \leq R+r, \\
L000908	\frac{4\pi}{3} \min[r,R]^3 & \text{if } d < |R-r|, \\
L000909	0 & \text{if } d > R+r,
L000910	\end{cases}
L000911	\end{equation}
L000912	where $\xi(x,y)=(1-x-y)^2(1+2(x+y)-3(x-y)^2)/16$.
L000913	The contribution from the remaining bubbles is approximated as an averaged quantity~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}
L000914	\begin{align}
L000915	\label{eq:F_k_second_1}
L000916	F_k^{(j> j_c)}(t) \simeq \textrm{exp}\left[-\sum^{+\infty}_{j=j_c+1}\int dt_jdd_j p_{t,j}(t_j;k)p_d(d_j;t_j,k)f(t;t_j,d_j,k) \right].
L000917	\end{align}
L000918	Approximating the sum over $j$ by an integral and the Poisson distribution in Eq.~\eqref{eq:poisson} by a delta function $p_{t,j}(t;k)=\dot{\overline{N}}_k(t)\delta_{D}\left(\overline{N}_k(t)-j\right)$, the expression in Eq.~\eqref{eq:F_k_second_1} simplifies to~\cite{Lewicki:2024ghw}
L000919	\begin{align}
L000920	\label{eq:F_k_second_2}
L000921	F_k^{(j> j_c)}(t)\simeq \textrm{exp}\left[-\int_{-\infty}^t d\tilde{t}d\tilde{d} ~\Theta\left(\overline{N}_k(\tilde{t})-j_c\right)\frac{d\overline{N}_k(\tilde{t})}{d\tilde{t}}p_d(\tilde{d};\tilde{t},k)f(t;\tilde{t},\tilde{d},k) \right].
L000922	\end{align}
