L000987	\subsection{Curvature power spectrum}
L000988	
L000989	As discussed in the main text, the curvature power spectrum $\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k)$ is related to the variance $\sigma_\mathcal{R}^2$of the spatially-averaged curvature by $\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k=R^{-1}) \simeq 2\sigma_\mathcal{R}^2/3\pi$.
L000990	Ref.~\cite{Elor:2023xbz} derived a semi-analytic expression for the curvature power spectrum generated during a FOPT, which we parametrize as
L000991	\begin{equation}
L000992	\label{eq:PR_FOPT}
L000993	\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k)
L000994	=
L000995	\left(\frac{\alpha_\star}{1+\alpha_\star}\right)^{\!2}
L000996	\left(\frac{H_\star}{\beta}\right)^{\!2}
L000997	\frac{a_1 (k_p/\beta)^3}{\bigl[a_2+(k_p/\beta)^2\bigr]^3}
L000998	\,\Theta(H_\star-k_p) ,
L000999	\end{equation}
L001000	where $k_p\equiv k/a_\star$, and the numerical coefficients are
L001001	$a_1\simeq0.90$ and $a_2\simeq0.24$.
L001002	The Heaviside function enforces the restriction to superhorizon modes, since subhorizon fluctuations lie outside the regime of validity of the effective description used in Ref.~\cite{Elor:2023xbz}. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Variance_perturbations} (right), the curvature power spectrum in Eq.~\eqref{eq:PR_FOPT} captures the correct order of magnitude and overall parametric dependence of the spectrum obtained in this work.
L001003	However, quantitative differences are clearly visible across the parameter range considered.
L001004	A detailed investigation of the origin of these discrepancies, including differences in approximations and domain of validity, is left for future work.
